{"t":1792523714,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"f46c3694-d326-473a-b75f-72c5342336cf","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-20T16:35:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 201635\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\nIssued by National Weather Service Mount Holly NJ\n1235 PM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes to the forecast.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Cool and dry weather continues through the weekend,\n  although a slow-moving Nor'easter off the coast will bring \n  gusty winds along the Chesapeake Bay and minor tidal flooding \n  at times through Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool and dry weather continues through the weekend,\nalthough a slow-moving Nor'easter off the coast will bring gusty\nwinds along the Chesapeake Bay and minor tidal flooding at times\nthrough Friday.\n\nSurface high pressure remains over the forecast area through  \nthe forecast period as a Nor'easter continues to drift offshore.\nA tight pressure gradient between surface high pressure nearby\nand the offshore Nor'easter will yield gusty conditions daily, \nmainly along and east of the I-95 corridor. Under high pressure,\ntemperatures moderate through Thursday with high temperatures \nin the 50s and 60s. Overnight low temperatures will be in the \n30s and 40s through Thursday night. Aloft, upper level troughing\ndevelops across the eastern CONUS as ridging develops over \ncentral CONUS. By Thursday, the Nor'easter is absorbed into the \nupper trough and tracks off the coast of New England. \n\nSurface high pressure builds southward out of southeastern\nCanada and eastern Great Lakes regions into the mid-Atlantic\nFriday through Monday. Dry conditions are expected each day with\na gradual moderating trend in temperatures. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /16Z TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nVFR conditions continue as surface high pressure builds overhead\nand dry conditions persist. Winds remain primarily out of the\nnorth each day with terminals near the airport gusting around 15\nknots in the afternoon. Otherwise, terminals can expect light\nnortherly winds, no precipitation, and VFR conditions. \n\nVFR conditions Friday through Saturday night. Winds generally north \n5 to 15 knots.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nThe Nor'easter off the Mid-Atlantic coast will deliver a \nprolonged period of SCA conditions (mainly over the \ncentral/lower waters). Small Craft Advisories remain in effect\nthrough tonight and will likely need to be extended. The threat\nis highest in the lower portions of the waters, with additional\nAdvisories possible through the end of the week. Otherwise, dry\nconditions and non-hazardous marine weather is expected. \n\nNortherly flow with building high pressure could allow for Small \nCraft Advisories through midday Friday, then winds diminish and no \nmarine hazards expected thereafter.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal anomalies will rise again through midweek as a Nor'easter\nstarts drifting northward offshore. Tidal locations including \nDahlgren, Annapolis, Solomons Island, and Alexandria are \nforecast to reach minor flood stage by Tuesday night or \nWednesday high tide cycles. Annapolis could approach moderate \nflood stage. However, some uncertainty remains on how exactly \nthis Nor'easter will affect the tide levels. Elevated water \nlevels likely won't decline until late in the week when the low \nwill finally start pulling out to sea.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Small Craft Advisory until 6 AM EDT Wednesday for MDZ008.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 6 AM EDT Wednesday for ANZ530>534-\n     537>543.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EDT this evening for ANZ535-\n     536.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...99\nAVIATION...99\nMARINE...99\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792523679,"days":{